  ok   -- and a FRESH PROCESS computes the identical tag (sidechain:ed8ba1cf), so a watchdog polling every few minutes can still tell one thread from another across its own runs -- which a per-interpreter salted hash would silently break

R1: reverting any fix on this branch must turn one of these RED
  ok   -- the fixture really does hold a literal U+2028 inside a JSON string, and json.loads parses all 3 of its lines -- so it is VALID JSONL, not a corrupt file whose mishandling would not matter
  ok   -- and str.splitlines() sees 4 lines in those 3 -- the alternative split this control exists to forbid does disagree, so the control is not vacuous
  ok   -- splitting the RAW BYTES on b'\n' reads 3 lines and 3 turns. Under str.splitlines() this file is 4 lines and 2 TURNS: a turn silently lost, with no error, on a transcript that is entirely well-formed
  ok   -- the line the trim drops is 286 bytes but 280 characters, so byte and character arithmetic MUST give different answers here
  ok   -- start_offset (553) is the TRUE byte position of the window's first kept line (553). Counting characters instead gives 547 -- an offset pointing into the middle of a line
  ok   -- and resuming from it returns the same 2 turns the window held -- which is the consequence that matters: a wrong offset drops the oldest turn on resume
  ok   -- the fixture genuinely has no trailing newline
  ok   -- a transcript with no trailing newline still reads all 3 lines and 3 turns -- popping the last element unconditionally would read 2, losing the newest turn on exactly the file shape a LIVE session always has
